Task 2: Custom Health Checks and Application Deployment
Step 2.1: Author Lua Health Check Logic and Register It with Argo CD
Argo CD evaluates resource health using Lua scripts stored in the argocd-cm ConfigMap. Each script receives the live Kubernetes object as obj and must return a table with status (one of Healthy, Progressing, Degraded, Missing, Suspended) and message.

Your health check contract for each resource type is:

-- Interface contract: every health script must satisfy this shape.
-- Input:  obj  (table) â the full live Kubernetes resource object
-- Output: hs   (table) â must contain exactly these two string fields

hs = {}
hs.status  = ""   -- one of: "Healthy" | "Progressing" | "Degraded" | "Missing" | "Suspended"
hs.message = ""   -- human-readable explanation shown in the Argo CD UI
return hs
Implement three scripts satisfying the following behavioral contracts:

apps_Deployment

Healthy when readyReplicas == replicas and replicas > 0
Degraded when readyReplicas == 0 and the Deployment has existed for more than its progressDeadlineSeconds
Progressing in all other transient states
Message must include numeric replica counts in all branches
v1_Service

Healthy for ClusterIP and NodePort types unconditionally (these have no external provisioning step)
Progressing for LoadBalancer type until status.loadBalancer.ingress is non-nil and non-empty
Healthy for LoadBalancer once ingress is populated, with the first ingress IP or hostname in the message
v1_ConfigMap

Healthy always; message must report the count of keys in obj.data
Register all three scripts by patching argocd-cm, then restart argocd-server and argocd-application-controller to apply the change. Confirm the restart completes cleanly.

# Patch argocd-cm with your Lua scripts.
# Replace the placeholder values below with your actual Lua implementations.
# The key naming convention is: resource.customizations.health.<group>_<Kind>
# where group "v1" maps to core API resources and "apps" maps to apps/v1.

kubectl patch configmap argocd-cm -n argocd --type merge --patch '
{
  "data": {
    "resource.customizations.health.apps_Deployment": "REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_LUA_SCRIPT",
    "resource.customizations.health.v1_Service":      "REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_LUA_SCRIPT",
    "resource.customizations.health.v1_ConfigMap":    "REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_LUA_SCRIPT"
  }
}'

kubectl rollout restart deployment/argocd-server deployment/argocd-application-controller -n argocd
kubectl rollout status deployment/argocd-server -n argocd --timeout=120s
kubectl rollout status deployment/argocd-application-controller -n argocd --timeout=120s
Confirmation: kubectl get configmap argocd-cm -n argocd -o jsonpath='{.data}' | jq 'keys' lists all three resource.customizations.health.* keys.

Step 2.2: Create a Git-Backed Application and Register It with Argo CD
Argo CD is a GitOps controller â it continuously reconciles cluster state against a Git repository. You will create a local bare Git repository (simulating a remote) containing Kubernetes manifests, then register it as an Argo CD Application.

Your application must include at minimum:

A Namespace manifest for health-demo
A Deployment of two replicas running nginx:1.25 with liveness and readiness probes on port 80
A ClusterIP Service selecting the Deployment pods
A ConfigMap with at least three data keys
Constraints:

The Argo CD Application must use syncPolicy.automated with selfHeal: true so that manual cluster edits are reverted automatically
CreateNamespace=true must be set in syncOptions so Argo CD creates the namespace if absent
The repoURL must point to the local bare repository path using the file:// scheme
# Scaffold â fill in the manifest content yourself
mkdir -p ~/argocd-health-demo/manifests
cd ~/argocd-health-demo

git init
git config user.name "Lab User"
git config user.email "lab@example.com"

# Create your manifests under manifests/ then:
