# Register the repo with Argo CD (no credentials needed for local file:// repos)
argocd repo add "file://${HOME}/argocd-health-demo.git"

# Create and apply your Application manifest, then verify
argocd app list
argocd app get health-demo-app
Confirmation: argocd app get health-demo-app shows Health Status: Healthy and Sync Status: Synced within two minutes of application creation.

Task 3: Notification Pipeline, Failure Injection, and Recovery Validation
Step 3.1: Deploy a Webhook Receiver and Wire Argo CD Notifications
Argo CD Notifications (a controller that watches Application objects and fires messages on state transitions) must be installed separately from core Argo CD. You will install it, configure a webhook service endpoint as the notification target, and subscribe the application to three triggers.

Your webhook receiver must satisfy this interface â implement it in any language or framework you choose:

class WebhookReceiver:
    def handle_post(self, headers: dict, body: bytes) -> tuple[int, str]:
        """
        Accept an HTTP POST from Argo CD Notifications.

        Parameters
        ----------
        headers : dict
            HTTP request headers; expect Content-Type: application/json
        body : bytes
            Raw JSON payload from Argo CD Notifications template

        Returns
        -------
        tuple[int, str]
            HTTP status code (200 on success) and response body string.
            Must append a timestamped record to a persistent log file at
            /tmp/webhook-alerts.log before returning.
        """
        ...
Architectural constraints:

The receiver must run as a Kubernetes Deployment of one replica in the default namespace
It must be reachable at http://webhook-receiver.default.svc.cluster.local:9000/notify
It must not require authentication (this is a lab environment)
Configure argocd-notifications-cm with:

A template named app-health-change that includes app.metadata.name, app.status.health.status, and app.status.sync.status in the payload
Three triggers: on-health-degraded (fires when health is Degraded), on-sync-succeeded (fires when sync is Synced), on-sync-failed (fires when sync is OutOfSync)
A webhook service definition pointing to your receiver URL
