# Official guide: https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/notifications/
kubectl apply -n argocd \
  -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/notifications_install/install.yaml

kubectl wait deployment/argocd-notifications-controller \
  -n argocd \
  --for=condition=Available \
  --timeout=120s
Troubleshoot this step:

Error seen: unable to recognize URL: no matches for kind "NotificationsController"
Recovery: The notifications manifest URL changes between Argo CD releases; fetch the correct URL from the release page at https://github.com/argoproj/argo-cd/releases and look for notifications_install.yaml
Docs: https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/notifications/
After deploying the receiver and applying argocd-notifications-cm, annotate the application to subscribe it to all three triggers:

# Annotation format: notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.<trigger-name>.<service-type>: <service-name>
# Apply all three annotations in a single patch to avoid race conditions.
kubectl patch application health-demo-app -n argocd --type merge --patch '
{
  "metadata": {
    "annotations": {
      "notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-health-degraded.webhook":  "health-webhook",
      "notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-sync-succeeded.webhook":   "health-webhook",
      "notifications.argoproj.io/subscribe.on-sync-failed.webhook":      "health-webhook"
    }
  }
}'
Confirmation: kubectl logs -n argocd deployment/argocd-notifications-controller --tail=20 shows no failed to send errors after the application reaches Synced.

Step 3.2: Inject Failures, Validate Detection, and Confirm Recovery
This step validates the entire pipeline end-to-end. You will introduce two categories of failure, observe that Argo CD detects them and fires notifications, then restore the application and confirm recovery propagation.

Failure scenario A â bad image tag: Update the Deployment manifest in your working repository to use nginx:this-tag-does-not-exist, commit the change, push it to the bare repository, and force an Argo CD sync. The Deployment will enter ImagePullBackOff, which your Lua health check must classify as Degraded.

Failure scenario B â replica starvation: After reverting the image, update the Deployment to request cpu: 8000m per container (more than any node can provide). Pods will remain Pending indefinitely. Your health check must classify this as Degraded as well.

For each scenario, confirm all three of the following before reverting:

# 1. Argo CD reports Degraded
argocd app get health-demo-app --output json | jq '.status.health'

# 2. Individual resource health reflects the failure
kubectl get application health-demo-app -n argocd \
  -o jsonpath='{range .status.resources[*]}{.kind}/{.name}: {.health.status} - {.health.message}{"\n"}{end}'

# 3. Webhook receiver logged the notification
kubectl exec deployment/webhook-receiver -- cat /tmp/webhook-alerts.log | tail -10
After confirming detection for both scenarios, restore the Deployment to nginx:1.25 with cpu: 250m, commit, push, and sync. Confirm the application returns to Healthy and that the on-sync-succeeded trigger fires a notification visible in the receiver log.

Confirmation: The receiver log at /tmp/webhook-alerts.log contains at minimum three timestamped entries â one Degraded entry per failure scenario and one Synced entry after recovery.

